WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.374 --> 00:00:03.030
- You look beautiful.
- Thank you so much.

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.440
I'm so happy to be here.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.790
- Oh my God, I'm super stoked.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.410
- It's my first time on your show.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:11.220
- Okay, and also, like I watch you so much

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.660
in everything you do.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.310
Literally, one of my
favorite jokes I make,

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.680
is "Zero Dark Thirty,"
it's when I go dark.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.270
I'm not lying, you're so good.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.619
- You are.
- Yeah.

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:20.610
- Thank you.
- No, she was literally,

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.650
I was referencing her
before the door opened.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.580
I didn't even say your name,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.170
and she was like, "It's coming." (claps)

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.300
She was so excited.
- Oh, my gosh.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.850
Well, I told everyone backstage,

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:30.900
I was a little nervous to meet you

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.107
'cause I just adore you so.

00:00:32.107 --> 00:00:33.000
- Oh, my gosh.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.170
Well, feeling is mutual.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.070
- There we go.
- All right.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.536
- Can we be friends?
- Well, we can be friends.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.070
We're friends. I'm gonna tell everyone.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.990
Okay. Congrats on your Oscar win again.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:41.478
- Thank you.
- That was incredible.

00:00:41.478 --> 00:00:42.311
(audience applauding)

00:00:42.311 --> 00:00:43.144
That was so cool.

00:00:43.144 --> 00:00:45.390
Do you have like a place you keep awards?

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.050
- Well, (chuckles) it's
in a, like an office room

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.060
on a bookshelf with-
- Oh, that's where my...

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.620
See, people make fun of me.
- [Jessica] Really.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.200
- But I feel like a tool
if I put it out, you know?

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.120
Now, one of my friends
is mad at me right now,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.020
'cause you walk in his house,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:59.280
and they're like right there.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.600
- Is there like a spotlight on?

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.694
You open the front door

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:02.527
and it looks like a shrine.
- It is.

00:01:02.527 --> 00:01:04.320
Literal it like a piano.
It's amazing, I love it.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.280
But it's so exciting.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.113
You earned it. You did it, I love it.

00:01:06.113 --> 00:01:07.680
- Thank you.
- Yeah.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:08.940
But this all kind of started out,

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:09.773
I didn't know this about you.

00:01:09.773 --> 00:01:10.950
So, you went to Julliard?

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.600
- Yeah.
- But you went on scholarship

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.760
that Robin Williams paid for?
- I know.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.530
What an incredible human
being that guy was.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.777
- I didn't know that.
- Yeah.

00:01:18.777 --> 00:01:21.060
- Did you meet him?
- I sadly did not.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.190
So, the scholarship was a huge deal.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.380
I'm the first person in my
family to go to college.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.970
It paid for all of my schooling.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.800
It paid for me to be able
to go home for Christmas

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.633
and see my family.

00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:31.440
All my books, my apartment, everything.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.590
And so, every year I wrote
a letter to thank him,

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:35.490
but I never got to meet him.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.040
And I was in L.A, I had a meeting

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.950
with a independent film
director at Fred Siegel

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.000
or something in the cafe.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.220
And I told him the story,

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:45.630
and all of a sudden, Robin Williams

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.850
walks into the restaurant area.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:48.683
And I was like, "Oh my gosh."

00:01:48.683 --> 00:01:49.830
And he goes, "You have to go say hi."

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:50.663
And it's like, "Okay, okay."

00:01:50.663 --> 00:01:51.990
And then I thought, "You know what?

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.480
I'm gonna let him enjoy his meal first.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:55.313
I don't wanna rush over there.

00:01:55.313 --> 00:01:56.730
- Oh, that's polite.
- Yeah.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:57.960
But then in the middle of the meal,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.670
he jumped up and ran out.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.340
So, I jumped up like
I was gonna chase him.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.050
And then I thought, "No,
I don't wanna scare him."

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.250
And I didn't go up to him.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.530
And I always regret that because I never

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.000
then had the opportunity to meet him.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.490
And I say to people, anytime
someone comes up to me,

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.590
I'm always like, "Thank
you for coming up."

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.090
Because this happened to me once

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.740
where I had an opportunity to meet someone

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.230
that I really wanted to talk
to and I didn't take it.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.640
So, now whenever someone comes up to me

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.080
I'm so happy to say hi to them.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.220
- Yeah, well and especially,
he probably would've loved that

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.690
to see where his money went.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:28.710
You know that's cool.
- After I tapped-

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.560
- You had a reason to go up.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.360
You weren't just like the
rest of us and had no reason,

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:34.800
like we're just fans.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.330
You had a really solid reason.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:37.530
I'm sorry you didn't get that.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:38.760
But it's a really good lesson though.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:39.600
- It is. It's a great lesson.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.900
- For all of us to really
take advantage of the moment.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.230
I just love this, because
you're so classy to me,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.600
and you're this amazing actress.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.340
And you love "Real Housewives."

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:51.173
- I'm obsessed.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.890
Do you not?
- What?

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.422
- Doesn't everyone love "Real Housewives?"

00:02:55.422 --> 00:02:56.438
(audience applauding)

00:02:56.438 --> 00:02:57.870
- I don't. I don't generally watch.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.180
So, what's your favorite
thing about it? Why?

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.200
My friends love it.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.730
- I like to find the characters on it.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.440
Like when I did "A Most Violent Year,"

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.730
I binged watched "The Real
Housewives of New Jersey."

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.080
So, all those hand gestures-

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:11.400
- I love that, that was your...

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.890
You know what I should do? "New Jersey."

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.510
- It was there. It was there.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.130
And what I'd love so much about the show,

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.230
is they take really eccentric people.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:20.063
Right?
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:20.063 --> 00:03:23.520
- And then, because by nature
of being on television,

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:24.870
it's all magnified.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.210
So, all these like really
interesting things about them,

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.080
are so much bigger.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.420
And I kinda steal it for my characters.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:31.530
- Oh, my gosh, that's incredible.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:32.363
What's your favorite one?

00:03:32.363 --> 00:03:33.330
There's a lot of them?

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:34.890
- I have so many favorites,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.960
but I think, of course,
"Beverly Hills" might be

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.550
the the OG for me.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.900
I love Garcelle.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.730
I'm obsessed with Garcelle, 'cause she,

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.440
I mean, we all just
need a friend like her.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.140
She's just so honest all the time.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.240
And I love her. I love her, she
doesn't care about anything.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.350
- All my friends are honest.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.343
I could use a liar.

00:03:50.343 --> 00:03:52.050
(audience laughing)

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:53.637
All my friends are really honest.

00:03:53.637 --> 00:03:54.540
So, I was like...

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.950
I'm just kidding, I love that.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.750
I heard that you like to
mess with people on set.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.760
Like you mess with, was it
James McAvoy that you did?

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:00.690
So, what did you do to him?

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.100
- So, this also it takes...

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.300
I've worked with James McAvoy.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.133
I've done three films with him,

00:04:04.133 --> 00:04:05.820
and he fun-
- You felt comfortable?

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:06.653
- I felt comfortable.

00:04:06.653 --> 00:04:08.940
You gotta really plan who to prank.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.530
I just also worked with Michael Shannon.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:11.713
Probably, not the guy to prank.

00:04:11.713 --> 00:04:12.660
(audience laughing)

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.543
Can you imagine that?
- Just letting you know.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.320
- So, yeah, we were shooting
"X-Men," and he is Professor X.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.600
And he's in the wheelchair, and
at some point he's on wires,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.730
and at some point he's being lifted up.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.040
And he does the scene and it's good.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.810
And I had planned this with the director,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.760
and the director said, "Okay, final take.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:30.750
Please do it again."

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.580
So, they lift him up and he's acting,

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.100
you know, like he's being
lifted by this magic,

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.360
or whatever it is.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.652
And all of a sudden, the
"Macarena" starts playing.

00:04:39.652 --> 00:04:40.620
(audience laughing)

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.020
And the guys that were holding the wires

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.020
were having him dance the "Macarena."

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.660
It was probably, there's no topping it.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.250
I think it was the best.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.930
- Yeah, that's awesome that
when you have friends like that,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.270
that you know you can prank
like that, and they'll lean in.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:55.290
- Oh yeah, he leaned in.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.220
In fact at one point,

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.750
'cause he was at the top of the stairs,

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.310
at one point, he kinda like grabbed me

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:00.990
and lifted me off the ground.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:02.070
And that's when I was
like, "Oh yeah, no, no.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.462
We gotta stop because
I could now get hurt."

00:05:04.462 --> 00:05:05.520
(audience laughing)

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:06.480
So, he was good fun.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:07.890
- Oh, I love that. That's fun.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.360
I wanna ask you about
something more serious,

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.160
because you went to a Ukraine recently,

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:11.993
right?
- [Jessica] Yeah.

00:05:11.993 --> 00:05:13.590
- And this, it's really
important to a lot of people.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.390
But why did you feel
like you wanted to go?

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.733
- I have a lot of friends
who are Ukrainian.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.720
I was talking to someone

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.790
when everything first started to happen.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.820
And I said how sad I was and asked,

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.530
said, "Please if there's anything

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.210
that I could ever do
to help, let me know."

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.160
And then, I was reached out to,

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.860
to ask if I would go and visit?

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.080
- Yeah.
- And I said, absolutely.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:38.850
It was a pretty intense thing.

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.200
I've never done anything like that before.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.790
I mean, I flew to Warsaw,

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.370
and then drove like 4.5
hours to a train station,

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.760
and then took a train for
12 hours to get to Kiev.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:49.260
- Wow.
- Yeah. You know?

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.390
And for the most of the
journey without a translator

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:54.150
and without security, I mean,
it was just me and my friend.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.790
And when I got there, I met
so many incredible people.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:59.310
I went to the children's hospital there.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.140
I mean, my heart's just breaking

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.390
with everything that
they're struggling with.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.200
I don't understand, every
time I open the news,

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.300
I'm just so sad about the things

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:10.410
that are happening in this world.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.803
You know, the stuff
that's happening in Iran.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.530
It's a constant sense of,

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.150
we really need to look out for each other,

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:19.380
and we really need to...

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:22.350
For me, I wanna use my platform to amplify

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.083
the voices of those that
are fighting for freedom.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:27.720
- And you get to tell their stories.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.670
That's what I love about movies too,

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.970
it's such a cool vessel
to tell people's stories.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:36.570
Which, by the way, I didn't
know "The Good Nurse"

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.300
was a true story until the very end.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.367
And my sister and I were like, "What?"

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:42.750
(panel laughing)

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:43.590
I didn't know it was a true...

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:44.767
Spoiler alert.

00:06:44.767 --> 00:06:45.600
(audience laughing)

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:46.433
But it's a true...

00:06:46.433 --> 00:06:47.266
It's already out there.

00:06:47.266 --> 00:06:48.240
I just was an idiot and didn't know.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.770
And I was just like, "What?"

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:50.610
So, But it's cool though

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:52.020
that you get to tell people's stories.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:53.430
I think that's powerful.
- Yeah.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.200
- Able to use your platform like that.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.160
Cause that impacts people.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.780
Art impacts people in a different way.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:06:58.696
- It does.
- Than seeing you on the news.

00:06:58.696 --> 00:07:01.800
I grew up wanting to know women's stories.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.290
In the history books, I
wasn't really learning

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:05.850
about women's heroism.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:07.980
And women are superheroes.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.020
- And so, learning about
all these true stories

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:13.170
of these incredible women,
and especially Amy Loughren,

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:16.777
I love to be able to
use my profession to go,

00:07:16.777 --> 00:07:17.940
"Oh, hey you guys,

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.280
I know there's all these
other superheroes out there,

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.630
but look at this woman right here."

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:23.190
And we could all be like that,

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:24.600
and we could all be inspired by her.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.730
- No, literally it blew my mind

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:28.350
when I found out it was real at the end.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:29.183
I didn't know.

00:07:29.183 --> 00:07:31.020
I literally went into it blind.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.060
I didn't know it's a
plot. I didn't, no idea.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:36.960
- And you know, a lot of gets
exaggerated in Hollywood.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.100
And I don't wanna give any spoilers,

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:40.650
but the last scene in the
movie really happened.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:41.970
It wasn't exaggerated.

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:44.760
- I can't believe it.
- It's insane.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.280
Our film really is, the media,

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.080
there's a lot of teaching the lesson

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.090
of using violence to stop violence.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:52.860
You always see the them going out there,

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.630
chasing the bad guy with the gun.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.030
And there's a shootout, and
then you get the bad guys.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.170
Our story really was like,

00:07:58.170 --> 00:07:59.760
well, what happens if this woman

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.890
uses compassion and empathy,

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.500
and reminds someone
that he's a human being

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.450
when other people are
treating him as a monster?

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.720
And that compassion is what
stops the cycle of violence.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.970
- And that empathy is the superpower.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:12.803
- [Jessica] Exactly.

00:08:12.803 --> 00:08:14.190
- Yeah, being empathetic
is the superpower.

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.830
So many messages there for
all of us to learn, obviously.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.470
But you two are incredible.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.319
I mean, I love you separately.
- Thank you.

00:08:21.319 --> 00:08:23.130
(audience applauding)

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.050
- It was like a masterclass in acting.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:28.590
Like even what your character
is going through physically,

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:30.420
while having this whole story happen,

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:34.920
and her family, and being a
single mom, it was just a lot.

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:36.150
I found it in incredible.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:38.400
- Yeah. So, Amy's a single
mom, the two daughters.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.050
And I mean, this is all true story.

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:42.360
She had suffered from cardiomyopathy,

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.550
which means she needed a heart transplant,

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:46.440
and she was working as a night nurse.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:47.580
Her heart was not allowed

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:50.070
to go above a certain beats per minute.

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.150
And all this is happening.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:52.710
Being a nurse is a very physical job,

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:53.820
people underestimate that.
- Yeah.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.160
- You're constantly carrying
people and turning them,

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.380
and you're running and doing compressions.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.210
Compressions are very physical.

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:02.520
And that was actually, that
was the best part for me,

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:03.810
is that when nurses saw the film

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:05.640
they said you got the compressions right.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:08.610
Because, I mean, we went to
nursing school, Eddie and I,

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.220
and I guess the whole thing,
a lot of times in movies,

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:13.290
you see people bend their wrist.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.030
- No.
- You're never supposed to.

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:16.423
Exactly, use your core.

00:09:16.423 --> 00:09:19.383
- Which I am so much of, so
that's why it was helpful.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.510
- But also you're-
- Covered in core.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:22.650
- In you're mind,

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:25.273
the whole time that you're
doing compressions, think,

00:09:25.273 --> 00:09:28.530
♪ Ah, ha, ha, ha, stayin'
alive, stayin' alive ♪

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:29.700
- Oh my god. Are you?

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:32.048
- Yes. And that's the
beat of the compressions.

00:09:32.048 --> 00:09:33.127
- Oh, my god, I didn't know that.

00:09:33.127 --> 00:09:33.960
(audience applauding)

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:34.793
- So, everyone's on-

00:09:34.793 --> 00:09:36.120
- My teacher did not teach me that.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.440
- Yeah, but that's the beat.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:38.760
That's how you're supposed to

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.080
- Oh my gosh.
- Yeah.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:41.517
- Look what you just taught
us. The more you know.

00:09:41.517 --> 00:09:43.500
So, you and Eddie Redmayne

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.020
you said y'all went to nurse school,

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:48.300
but I heard that Eddie
didn't do so great in it.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.730
I heard that he wasn't like super great.

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:52.080
- Here's the thing.
That's what Eddie says.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.720
He is probably the kindest
person I've ever met.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:56.889
And the most polite, the
British, very polite.

00:09:56.889 --> 00:09:58.380
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:01.710
- I do have that kind of
American cowboy mentality

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:03.067
where someone shows me
something and I'm like,

00:10:03.067 --> 00:10:03.987
"Yeah, let me try it."

00:10:03.987 --> 00:10:06.030
So, I would just jump on in,

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.970
trying to give a mannequin, and an IV,

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:11.250
and Eddie's like, "Oh, hold on."

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.320
And he's really nervous about it.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:14.670
So, what we came to realize

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:16.740
after our nursing school in our film,

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:19.533
is that neither one of
us would be a good nurse.

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:22.620
I mean, you stick to the professionals,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.690
because I mean, I can look like it,

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:25.980
I can be very confident

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:28.260
while I'm doing it
- You definitely look like it.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:31.080
- But no, I'm not the
one you want an IV from.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:34.260
- Yeah. I don't think
that's my calling either.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.420
You're also, I have to mention
this 'cause I'm a musician.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:37.253
I love this, I'm excited about it.

00:10:37.253 --> 00:10:39.390
